As at 2026-11-06, under Singapore law, the conduct that amounts to hijacking an aircraft is defined in section 3 of the Hijacking of Aircraft and Protection of Aircraft and International Airports Act 1978 [CITE:6]. Hijacking is committed when a person unlawfully and intentionally seizes or exercises control of an aircraft in service by the use of force or by threats of any kind, or by any technological means. This applies regardless of the nationality or citizenship of the person, the State in which the aircraft is registered, and whether the aircraft is in Singapore or elsewhere [CITE:6].

Regarding the powers of the commander of an aircraft in flight, section 5 of the Tokyo Convention Act 1971 [CITE:14] grants the commander certain powers when they have reasonable grounds to believe that a passenger on board the aircraft is endangering the safety of the aircraft or persons or property on board. The commander may take reasonable measures, including restraint of the person's person, as may be necessary:

- To protect the safety of the aircraft or of persons or property on board;
- To maintain good order and discipline on board the aircraft;
- To enable the commander to disembark or deliver that person in accordance with subsection (8) [CITE:14].

Additionally, the commander may require or authorise any member of the crew or any passenger to render assistance in restraining the person [CITE:14]. The commander may also take measures against any person on board the aircraft without being authorised, if they have reasonable grounds to believe that such measures are immediately necessary to protect the safety of the aircraft or persons or property on board [CITE:14].
